        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://OntoTCM.org.cn/ontologies/tcm_v38/accompanies"/>
        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://OntoTCM.org.cn/ontologies/tcm_v38/f#ClinicalFinding"/>
      </owl:Restriction>
    </rdfs:subClassOf>
    <tcm:definition xml:lang="zh-cn">不同程度的听力减退，甚至听觉丧失，不闻外声。见于风热袭肺证、肝火亢盛证、肝阳上亢证、肝血虚证、肾阴虚证、肾气不足证、心肾不交证、脾胃气虚证、气滞血瘀证等。又称失听。突然耳聋见于血瘀耳窍证、痰火闭耳证、肝火燔耳证，或气厥、风眩、耵耳、异物入耳、暴聋耳鸣、脑瘤、耳眩晕等，又称耳暴聋。耳渐聋多见于精气虚衰，不能上充清窍，属虚证。间歇耳聋见于肝阳上亢证、痰湿中阻证、肝肾阴虚证、阴阳两虚证，或风眩、神劳、虚眩、血劳、髓劳等。</tcm:definition>
    <tcm:definition xml:lang="en">Hearing loss of varying degree, up to complete loss of hearing with insensibility to external sounds. Seen in wind-heat invading the lung pattern, hyperactive liver fire pattern, liver yang hyperactivity pattern, liver blood deficiency pattern, kidney yin deficiency pattern, kidney qi insufficiency pattern, heart-kidney non-interaction pattern, spleen-stomach qi deficiency pattern, or qi stagnation with blood stasis pattern. Also known as hearing loss (shiting). Sudden deafness is seen in blood stasis in the ear orifice pattern, phlegm-fire blocking the ear pattern, liver fire flaring the ear pattern, or qi syncope, wind dizziness, cerumen obstruction, foreign body in the ear, sudden deafness with tinnitus, brain tumor, or ear vertigo; also called sudden ear deafness. Gradual deafness is mostly due to debilitation of essential qi failing to fill the clear orifices upward, pertaining to deficiency patterns. Intermittent deafness is seen in liver yang hyperactivity pattern, phlegm-dampness obstructing the middle pattern, liver-kidney yin deficiency pattern, yin-yang dual deficiency pattern, or wind dizziness, spirit taxation, deficiency dizziness, blood taxation, or marrow taxation.</tcm:definition>
    <tcm:reference>Book(中医临床常见症状术语规范)、 8.1.6.2.33</tcm:reference>
    <tcm:synonym>失听</tcm:synonym>
    <tcm:hpoID>HP:0012714</tcm:hpoID>
    <tcm:severityText>中度至重度</tcm:severityText>
    <tcm:frequencyText>偶尔发作</tcm:frequencyText>
    <tcm:triggerText>环境刺激</tcm:triggerText>
    <tcm:reliefText>情绪调节</tcm:reliefText>
    <tcm:snomedCT>15188001</tcm:snomedCT>
    <tcm:snomedCTTerm>Hearing loss</tcm:snomedCTTerm>
    <tcm:icd11>AB52</tcm:icd11>
    <tcm:icd11Term>Deafness</tcm:icd11Term>
  
    <oboInOwl:id>TCM-SO:sx_耳聋</oboInOwl:id>
    <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>TCM_symptom</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
    <obo:IAO_0000115>Hearing loss of varying degree, up to complete loss of hearing with insensibility to external sounds. Seen in wind-heat invading the lung pattern, hyperactive liver fire pattern, liver yang hyperactivity pattern, liver blood deficiency pattern, kidney yin deficiency pattern, kidney qi insufficiency pattern, heart-kidney non-interaction pattern, spleen-stomach qi deficiency pattern, or qi stagnation with blood stasis pattern. Also known as hearing loss (shiting). Sudden deafness is seen in blood stasis in the ear orifice pattern, phlegm-fire blocking the ear pattern, liver fire flaring the ear pattern, or qi syncope, wind dizziness, cerumen obstruction, foreign body in the ear, sudden deafness with tinnitus, brain tumor, or ear vertigo; also called sudden ear deafness. Gradual deafness is mostly due to debilitation of essential qi failing to fill the clear orifices upward, pertaining to deficiency patterns. Intermittent deafness is seen in liver yang hyperactivity pattern, phlegm-dampness obstructing the middle pattern, liver-kidney yin deficiency pattern, yin-yang dual deficiency pattern, or wind dizziness, spirit taxation, deficiency dizziness, blood taxation, or marrow taxation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
    <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>失听</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
    <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>HPO:HP:0012714</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0012714"/>
    <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>SCTID:15188001</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://snomed.info/id/15188001"/>
    <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ICD11:AB52</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
  
